Z||000014||the need for speed||12-13-2001||11:32 AM||||I have koni front and rear shocks with eibock springs. now if I want to go with coilovers can I keep my koni, and if so what do you have to do to make them work together?||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierochild||12-13-2001||11:41 AM||||Before any meaningful decision can be made, your goal must be stated.  Replacing worn out parts, lowering the car, improving handleing, racing road or autocross, or a combination of these.  Each of these has a different answer.  I replaced my weak rear struts with the Held KYB coil-over setup.  I chose to go with the 300lb spring rate to give a slight handleing improvement.  The ride is a little firmer.  Compares to a stock Corvette, but handleing definately improved.  But then my originals were pretty well gone.  I am currently rethinking my setup.  Autocross IE handling has become my primary goal.  I plan on changing both front and rear springs with springs as firm as I can tolerate on the street.  Even this will be a compromise over an all out race setup.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierochildsig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Dough19||12-13-2001||11:46 AM||||If you already have koni's and a 84-87 you can go to <A HREF="http://www.ground-control.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ground-control.com/</A>  Ground Control has eibach coilover kits for koni's. If you have an 88 though you might want to try   <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178/Coilover.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178/Coilover.html</A>   they have a ground control setup for the 88.<BR>||||reg||1
